   <text:p text:style-name="P2"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P2"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P17">Network topology essay: </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16">(Note: this essay is mostly based on the notes on one of my classmates. <text:span text:style-name="T6">The notes not being complete</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T7"><text:note text:id="ftn1" text:note-class="footnote"><text:note-citation>1</text:note-citation><text:note-body>
       <text:p text:style-name="P18">For example, I do not know which topology was chosen for each round. </text:p></text:note-body></text:note></text:span><text:span text:style-name="T6">, I could not do the whole assignment. I will focus more on the next homework. </text:span>) </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P16"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19">During the game, multiple network topologies were <text:span text:style-name="T8">re</text:span>created using the students as nodes <text:span text:style-name="T9">or bridges</text:span>. <text:span text:style-name="T8">Handwritten messages were then sent in a particular way at each round of the game, denoting the characteristics of the chosen network structure. Every time the message reached a new node, the student in charge of this “node” had to </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">reinterpret</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"> the message in a given amount of time, then send it. </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">Bridges would take messages from one node to another, reading the message, but not modifying it.</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"> </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">Th</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8">e final answer </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T9">was</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T8"> determined using a pre-selected consensus mechanism, that evolved throughout the rounds. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P19"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20">Similarly to a real network, some nodes were unreliable, as their transmissions were significantly different from the original message. The time allotted could be to short for correct reinterpretation, leading to summarized or truncated messages. The unreliability steams from the practical circumstances, not particularly from malevolent behavior. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P20"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21">About the consensus mechanisms of the game, we recognize some methods used in actual mainstream blockchains. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P21"><text:soft-page-break/>The first consensus was “The first message to arrive is the truth”. It shares similarities with the Solana consensus, which is a combination of the Proof-of-Stake (PoS) and Proof-of-History(PoH). <text:span text:style-name="T10">Showing work for consensus is the main principle of Proof-of-Work (PoW), the consensus mechanism of the BTC blockchain. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22">Voting is akin to the Ethereum blockchain. Two thirds of the voters have to agree on a final decision. <text:span text:style-name="T11">In the game, the group of voters could be limited </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T12">to the bridges. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23">To choose a network structure and a consensus mechanism for a blockchain, multiple aspects should be considered. Using our experiences from the simulation game, let’s see the pros and cons of the different options. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23">A line network is direct, with each node only sending the message to one other node. It is however very sensitive to node failure, as only one failure in the line fully prevents the recovery of the initial message. <text:s/></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23">A <text:span text:style-name="T13">mesh network is more complex </text:span><text:span text:style-name="T14">and slower</text:span><text:span text:style-name="T13"> as each node will have to send a copy of the message to multiple nodes. However, it helps to recover the original message in case of failure of one or multiple nodes, depending on the consensus mechanism. It is thus more robust to node failure. </text:span></text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P24">The main cause of error in the game seems to be the time given to each node to transmit the message correctly, especially when multiple copies had to be sent (mesh structure). There is a real tradeoff between speed, accuracy and robustness to node failure. </text:p>
   <text:p text:style-name="P23"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22"/>
   <text:p text:style-name="P22"/>
  </office:text>
 </office:body>
